# Testing Standards

## Unit vs integration

| Prefer unit | Prefer integration |
|-------------|-------------------|
| Pure logic, parsing, validation | Real databases, queues, HTTP servers, or OS services |
| JSON and files under `tmp_path` | Permissions, catalogs, wire protocols, subprocess to a real binary |
| CLI parsing, env guards, exit codes (monkeypatch **third-party** only) | Subprocess wiring, real binary on `PATH`, real external responses |
| String or file checks on bundled artifacts you ship | End-to-end paths through those artifacts |

- Mirror `src/` and `packages/` under `tests/unit/` and `tests/integration/`. Path and marker must match (`@pytest.mark.unit` / `@pytest.mark.integration`; also `@pytest.mark.slow`, `@pytest.mark.api` in `pytest.ini`).
- Do not reimplement an external system in unit tests (fake SQL engines, fake HTTP servers that mirror production). Inject at your boundary or use the real engine in integration (e.g. `sqlite3.connect(":memory:")` + migrations when the app uses SQLite). Prefer one integration test on observable state over a large fake.
- prek runs unit tests only. Integration runs in CI when enabled: add the same dependencies in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (service containers, CLIs, env). Uncomment workflow `on:` to turn CI on. `pytest.skip()` when a dep is missing locally; CI must not skip on main.

## Style

- No docstrings or comments on tests. Names: `test_<subject>_<expected_behavior>_when_<condition>`.
- In unit tests, use `unittest.mock` or `pytest-mock` on third-party boundaries, not real API/DB I/O.

## What to Test (and What Not To)

- Do not test language features or stdlib behavior (dataclass defaults, dict construction, import availability, `callable()` checks). Test only logic, constraints, and integrations _we_ defined. If removing the test would not risk a regression in _our_ code, delete it.
- Test behavior, not implementation. Prioritize validation, edge cases, and error handling. Use `parametrize` for boundary values.

## Forbidden Patterns

- No `monkeypatch.setattr` on first-party modules (`monkeypatch.setattr(my_package.foo, "bar", fake)` couples tests to import topology). Patching is OK on stdlib or third-party (e.g. `time.sleep`).
- No `importlib.import_module` as a patch anchor. Import directly; test purely in unit or with a real integration fixture (e.g. shared `conn`).
- No tests whose only assertion is that function A called collaborator B with arguments X. Assert observable effects through the public API.
